# Gotchas only

This is systematic pattern detection and contradiction spotting with evidence fraud analysis. It is not argument synthesis. Do not assemble a party's case, pair competing recitals as impeachment, or write advocacy.

A gotcha is a claim checked against real evidence (sworn, documentary, or an entered court act) that passes all four: same nameable fact, evidence runs AGAINST the claim, material to an element/procedure/credibility/damages, and both quotes are in the pulled text. Two motions or a claim vs a proposed order is advocacy, not a gotcha. A model pass without those four completed steps does not ship. When a pair is ambiguous, suppress it.

Related documents that show a defect about the same subject often SUPPORT a denial. That is not a gotcha. Shared topic, person, or caption is not the same fact. Evidence that restates the claim (not settled / case not settled; I live at X / a record lists X) is FOR, not against.

He-said-she-said is not a gotcha. A denial versus the other side's competing recital (including a bare service denial versus an affidavit of service, or a service-attempt claim versus a DMV/address printout that lists or supports the same address) is the ordinary dispute. The gotcha is when that party produces evidence and that evidence is fraudulent or false — their exhibit undercuts the claim, or the fraud/forensic check flags the exhibit they offered. Bought bank/utility/invoice templates filled in, printed, and scanned are exhibit fraud to look for. Court filings are out of scope for that check.

Recorded public instruments (mortgages, deeds, assignments) are not bank statements. Do not treat rounding, loan-math, or similar numeric quirks on those records as findings unless there is a genuine alteration or fraud signal.

Do not hardcode practice-area phrases. Types are only: factual contradiction, chronology inconsistency, material omission, procedural/foundational, admission against interest.

Risk scores from verified findings only — never document type or title.
